# Git Code Review settings for the sample scripts.
#
# 1. Copy this file to .env       cp .env.example .env
# 2. Lock it so only you can read it   chmod 600 .env
# 3. Fill in ONE of the token lines below.
#
# Never commit .env.  The .gitignore in this kit already keeps it out.
# In GitHub Actions do not use this file at all: put the token in a
# repository secret named GCR_TOKEN and the address in a variable named GCR_URL.

# Your server's API address.  The copy downloaded from your server has it
# filled in already.
GCR_URL=https://your-gcr-server.example.com/api/v1

# Best: the token stays encrypted on disk, and is decrypted only when a
# script runs.  Make the encrypted file with:  bash bash/gcr-token-store.sh save
# GCR_TOKEN_COMMAND=gpg --quiet --decrypt ~/.config/gcr/token.gpg

# Or read it from your system's keychain, for example:
#   macOS:  GCR_TOKEN_COMMAND=security find-generic-password -w -s gcr-token
#   Linux:  GCR_TOKEN_COMMAND=secret-tool lookup service gcr-token

# Or a file holding only the token, readable only by you (chmod 600).
# GCR_TOKEN_FILE=~/.config/gcr/token

# Last resort: the token itself.  Only on a machine nobody else uses.
# GCR_TOKEN=

# Only if your server uses a certificate from an internal company CA.
# GCR_CA_BUNDLE=/path/to/company-ca.pem
